<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="29" />
    <!--
        Disabling requestLegacyExternalStorage, hence enabling scoped storage on Android 10, would
        be an awful idea for user experience:
        - It would start requiring SAF for reading & writing non-media files on external storage,
          and for reading non-media files on removable storage. This is a problem because the
          definition of non-media files includes subtitle, lyric and playlist files in Android 10.
        - There's no way even for media files to batch-edit files. All other Android versions have
          some way to create one request for a batch of music files (at least one request per volume
          which is often still fine for UX). But Android Q requires one request per file, which
          means deleting 2000 files requires the user to click "Allow" 2000 times.
        - Unlike Android R's MediaStore that includes every file (if you include pending), Android Q
          can still suffer from files not being indexed. If that happens, you as app have no way to
          recover if requestLegacyExternalStorage is false, because File API access is denied.
        - Android Q has a bug that requires you to be able to edit song file when adding it to
          playlist which a music player would commonly not have under the scoped storage model.
          requestLegacyExternalStorage indirectly workarounds this bug because you always have write
          access (this bug was fixed in Android R).

        Only apps that are so simple they don't need this library would be usable with
        requestLegacyExternalStorage="false" without UX regressions, and considering using
        requestLegacyExternalStorage="false" has no advantages at all, enforce it to be enabled for
        this library.
    -->
    <application android:requestLegacyExternalStorage="true" tools:ignore="UnusedAttribute">
        <activity android:name=".WriteRequestActivity"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
            android:allowTaskReparenting="false"
            android:exported="false" />
        <activity android:name=".DeleteRequestActivity"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@style/PickerDialogTheme"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
            android:allowTaskReparenting="false"
            android:exported="false" />
        <activity android:name=".ManagerRequestActivity"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
            android:allowTaskReparenting="false"
            android:exported="false"
            tools:targetApi="30" />
    </application>
</manifest>